---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Macros are the three nutrients that give you calories: protein, carbohydrate, and fat. To
      calculate them, start from your daily calorie target, decide what share comes from each, then convert
      to grams. Protein and carbs give 4 calories per gram, fat gives 9. So 20 percent of a 2,000
      calorie day as protein is 400 calories, which is 100 grams. This calculator does the arithmetic
      for balanced, high-protein, low-carb, and keto splits, so you can see the grams each one produces
      side by side.
      This macronutrient calculator, sometimes called an IIFYM calculator or a protein carb fat calculator,
      turns your protein carbs fat targets into grams, including keto macros and the right amount of dietary fat.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try The Macro Calculator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="what-are-macros">
    <h2>What Macros Actually Are <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Every calorie you eat comes from one of three macronutrients. Protein builds and repairs tissue,
      including muscle, and keeps you full. Carbohydrate is your body's quick fuel, especially for hard
      training. Fat supports hormones and helps you absorb certain vitamins. Because each does a
      different job, how you divide your calories between them changes how you feel and perform, even
      when the calorie total stays the same. That division is your macro split.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="calories-to-grams">
    <h2>Turning Calories Into Grams <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The conversion rests on one fact: protein and carbohydrate each supply 4 calories per gram, while
      fat supplies 9. So the steps are always the same. Start from your calorie target, multiply by the
      percentage for a macro to get its calories, then divide by that macro's calories per gram. On a
      balanced 2,000 calorie day at 50 percent carbs, that is 1,000 carb calories divided by 4, or 250
      grams. Fat's higher energy density is why a keto split, heavy on fat, produces far fewer total
      grams of food than a balanced one at the same calories.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="choosing-a-split">
    <h2>Choosing a Split <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      There is no single correct ratio when you choose a macro split for a goal. Comparing balanced
      versus keto versus high-protein splits shows why different diets give very different fat and carb
      grams at the same calories. A balanced split suits general health and everyday activity.
      A higher-protein split helps with fat loss and muscle retention because protein is filling and
      spares muscle in a deficit. Low-carb and keto shift fuel toward fat and can suit some people's
      appetite and energy, though they are not magic. The best split is the one you can follow
      consistently while hitting your calories, so start with the preset closest to your goal and adjust
      by feel.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Calories first, macros second">
      <p>Your calorie total drives weight change. Hitting the calorie total matters more than a perfect ratio, so macros only fine-tune performance, fullness, and body composition. Get the total right, then let the split do the rest.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Obsessing over the exact ratio">
      <p>
        Landing within about ten grams of each target is plenty. Chasing a perfect split to the gram
        while ignoring your calorie total is effort spent on the wrong lever.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Forgetting fat is 9 calories a gram">
      <p>
        Fat packs more than twice the calories of protein or carbs per gram. A small amount of extra
        oil or nut butter adds up fast, which is why fat grams look small even on a high-fat split.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="Start from your TDEE">
      <p>Macros need a calorie target to divide. Estimate yours with the TDEE calculator first, then split it here. Everything runs in your browser and never leaves your device.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
